9. References and Bibliography
•  Kimes, S. E. (2004). "Restaurant Revenue Management: Implementation at Segovia." Cornell Hospitality Quarterly, Vol. 45(1), pp. 52-63.
•	Focus: Studied the economic impact of table allocation, time slot management, and reducing guest wait times during peak hours.

•  Hwang, J., & Lambert, C. U. (2008). "The Impact of Restaurant Reservation Policies on Customer Demands and No-show Behavior." International Journal of Hospitality Management, Vol. 27(4), pp. 573-581.
•	Focus: Analyzed customer responses to reservation deposits and how automated reminders reduce unannounced cancellations.



10. Conclusion

The Restaurant Table Reservation System effectively automate manual dine-in bookings. By adopting a frontend-first approach, the platform delivers an effective interface for both customers and admins before connecting to a stable backend.

Key Innovations and Standout Features:
•	Interactive Table Selection: Allows guests to view a live floor layout and pick their exact preferred table, rather than just choosing a random time slot.
•	Upfront Deposits via Stripe: Secures bookings by collecting a small deposit at the time of reservation, which significantly reduces unannounced cancellations (no-shows).
•	Pre-Order and Waitlist Automation: Links pre-selected meals directly to the kitchen upon booking confirmation and automatically notifies waitlisted users when a table opens up.



